window.docContents[60].resolve({"/Tier-2___-definitions/Symbolic___Specifications___/Symbolic.Specifications.Concrete.MachineEvents/#file-Symbolic___Specifications___Concrete___MachineEvents":{"contents":"Paper › Symbolic/Specifications/Concrete › MachineEvents.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the symbolic proof 4 headline theorems depend on it 26 of 64 lines trusted source ↗trust banner in the header: none; the closure computes B2 for the symbolic proofRead the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nCited in the paper* Hardware Events, §B.2: RunEvent, EnvEvent\n\nConcrete Byte Strings and CLI Trace EventsConcrete byte strings (HwBytes) and the event alphabet (CliEvent) for signal-session CLI execution traces.\n\n\n\nabbrev HwBytesL26–27 ↗#Concrete byte string, as opposed to symbolic DY.Bytes terms.\n\n\n\nstructure RunEventL36–43 ↗#One signal-session CLI invocation: caller party, command cmd, input bytes (bundle blob for init, stdin otherwise), coins file, and exit (some (code, stdout) or none on abort).\n\ninductive EnvEventL45–53 ↗#Environment events emitted between CLI runs: directory secret leaks, primitive breaks, public-key inversions, and corrupt identity registrations.\n\ndef EnvEvent.IsBreakInvertL55–58 ↗#Whether an environment event is a primitive break or key inversion.\n\ninductive CliEventL60–62 ↗#\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"Symbolic/Specifications/Concrete/MachineEvents.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/Symbolic___Specifications___/Symbolic.Specifications.Concrete.MachineEvents/#file-Symbolic___Specifications___Concrete___MachineEvents"}});